---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Larger Than Life — Daf Yomi Game: Chullin 90"
  description="Chullin 90: route the sciatic nerve of each offering to the right place on the altar, catch the Sages red-handed exaggerating with the number three hundred across the ash-mound, the golden vine, and the Curtain, then follow the Gemara's own back-and-forth over whether Rabbi Yehuda was certain or just guessing which thigh is forbidden."
>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Frank+Ruhl+Libre:wght@400;700;900&display=swap"
  />

  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#080a14] !text-[#dfe6f7]">
    <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
    <div class="border-b border-[#1c2648] bg-gradient-to-b from-[#10152c]/80 to-transparent px-4 pt-10 pb-8 text-center">
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] !text-[#7fa2e8]">
        <a href="/daf" class="hover:!text-[#a9c4ff]">daf yomi arcade</a> · chullin 90 · 2026-07-29
      </p>
      <h1 class="frank text-4xl font-black tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">📏 Larger Than Life</h1>
      <p class="frank mt-1 text-xl !text-[#e8a34d]" dir="rtl" lang="he">דִּבְּרוּ חֲכָמִים לְשׁוֹן הֲוַאי</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-1 text-xs italic !text-[#8fa0c9]">"The Sages spoke in exaggerated language" — Rabbi Ami, Chullin 90b:11</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c3cde6]">
        90b lets Rava and Shmuel do something startling: catch the Mishnah itself exaggerating. Three Temple
        wonders — an ash-mound, a golden vine, a woven curtain — each get one hyperbolic detail buried among real
        measurements. Find it. Then route seven real cases of the sciatic nerve through the altar's own back
        rooms, and follow the Gemara as it argues with itself over a single word: was Rabbi Yehuda <em>sure</em>,
        or just guessing?
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 pb-16 pt-8">
      <!-- ============ Context card ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#1c2648] bg-[#10152c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#7fa2e8]">before you play</p>
        <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#161e3a] bg-[#0b0f20] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#a9c4ff]">🦵 which prohibition came first?</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a2c9]">
              90a opens mid-question: a sacrificial animal's own sanctity should predate the gid hanasheh
              prohibition, so why does the mishna need to say gid applies to it at all? The answer lands on a
              bechor — a firstborn sanctified only as it leaves the womb, after the gid is already forbidden
              (Chullin 90a:1–7).
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#161e3a] bg-[#0b0f20] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#a9c4ff]">🍖 eaten offerings vs. burnt ones</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a2c9]">
              Rabbi Chiyya bar Yosef: gid hanasheh applies only to sacrifices that get eaten. Rabbi Yochanan: it
              applies to burnt offerings too. Rav Pappa and Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak each try to explain what's
              really at stake (Chullin 90a:8–90b:3).
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#161e3a] bg-[#0b0f20] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#a9c4ff]">🔥 the ash-mound's other job</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a2c9]">
              Rav Huna: the gid hanasheh of a burnt-offering doesn't burn with the rest of the animal — it's
              removed and set on the tapuach, the round mound of ash at the altar's center (Chullin 90b:4–8).
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#161e3a] bg-[#0b0f20] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#a9c4ff]">📢 when the Sages exaggerate</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a2c9]">
              That very tapuach appears again as "guzma" — Rava calls its 300-kor description an exaggeration, and
              Shmuel names two more: a golden vine and the Sanctuary's Curtain (Chullin 90b:9–15).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Score bar ============ -->
      <div class="sticky top-0 z-10 mb-6 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-5 gap-y-1 rounded-xl border border-[#1c2648] bg-[#080a14]/95 px-4 py-2.5 text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] backdrop-blur">
        <span class="!text-[#8fa0c9]">progress:</span>
        <span id="score-route" class="!text-[#7fa2e8]">routed 0/7</span>
        <span id="score-guzma" class="!text-[#e8a34d]">guzma 0/3</span>
        <span id="score-thigh" class="!text-[#7fa2e8]">thigh steps 0/5</span>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Activity 1: Route the Gid ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#1c2648] bg-[#10152c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#7fa2e8]">activity 1 · route the gid — Chullin 90a:8–90b:8</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c3cde6]">
          Seven real cases from the daf. For each, decide where it goes: does it <strong>ascend</strong> with the
          rest of the offering, does it land on the <strong>ash-mound</strong> (a fate reserved for one specific
          case), or does it come <strong>off the altar</strong> entirely?
        </p>
        <div id="route-list" class="mt-4 space-y-3"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Activity 2: Spot the 300 ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#1c2648] bg-[#10152c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#e8a34d]">activity 2 · spot the 300 — Chullin 90b:9–15</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c3cde6]">
          Three real mishnayot describe Temple wonders in precise numbers. In each, exactly one claim is
          <strong>guzma</strong> — deliberate exaggeration, flagged by Rava or Shmuel — hiding among real
          measurements. Click the one that isn't meant literally.
        </p>
        <div id="guzma-rounds" class="mt-4 space-y-4"></div>
        <div id="guzma-bonus" class="mt-4 hidden rounded-xl border border-[#3a2a10] bg-[#1a1206] p-4">
          <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide !text-[#e8a34d]">reveal · Rabbi Ami's rule — Chullin 90b:11–13</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#e0cdb0]">
            "The Torah spoke in exaggerated language [lashon havai], the Prophets spoke in exaggerated language,
            and the Sages spoke in exaggerated language." Torah: "cities great and fortified up to heaven"
            (Deuteronomy 9:1) — the walls never touched the sky. Prophets: at Shlomo's coronation, "the earth was
            split by the sound" of the celebration (I Kings 1:40) — the ground never actually cracked. And a
            bonus fourth case Rava flagged just before Shmuel's list: the daily lamb was given water to drink
            "from a golden cup" before slaughter, to make it easier to flay (Tamid 30a) — also guzma, not a
            literal vessel.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Activity 3: One Thigh or Two ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#1c2648] bg-[#10152c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#7fa2e8]">activity 3 · one thigh or two? — Chullin 90b:16–21</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c3cde6]">
          The mishna forbids gid hanasheh in both thighs. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees — only one, "and the mind decides
          for the right." Was he certain, or guessing? Reveal the Gemara's own investigation, step by step.
        </p>
        <div id="thigh-steps" class="mt-4 space-y-3"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Learn the real daf ============ -->
      <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-[#1c2648] bg-[#10152c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#7fa2e8]">learn the real daf</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c3cde6]">
          90a opens still inside the previous daf's question: since a sacrificial animal's own sanctity precedes
          the sciatic-nerve prohibition chronologically, why does the mishna even need to say gid hanasheh applies
          to such an animal? After a few rejected answers, the Gemara lands on a bechor — a firstborn animal that
          becomes sanctified only as it leaves the womb, meaning the gid prohibition (already in force since
          Yaakov wrestled the angel, Genesis 32:25–33) gets there first
          (<a class="!text-[#a9c4ff] underline hover:!text-[#cddcff]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.90a.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">90a:1–7</a>).
          A new discussion follows: Rabbi Chiyya bar Yosef holds gid hanasheh applies only to sacrifices that are
          eaten, while Rabbi Yochanan applies it even to burnt offerings that are entirely consumed on the altar —
          and the Gemara works through several ways to locate exactly where that dispute bites, including a close
          reading of "and the priest shall make it all smoke on the altar" against "the flesh and the blood"
          (<a class="!text-[#a9c4ff] underline hover:!text-[#cddcff]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.90a.8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">90a:8–90b:3</a>).
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c3cde6]">
          90b turns practical: Rav Huna rules that a burnt-offering's sciatic nerve is removed and placed on the
          tapuach, the ash-mound at the altar's center, rather than burned with the rest of the animal — confirmed
          by a baraita that also sends a peace-offering's sciatic nerve to the courtyard drain
          (<a class="!text-[#a9c4ff] underline hover:!text-[#cddcff]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.90b.4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">90b:4–8</a>).
          That same ash-mound then anchors one of the Talmud's most delightful digressions: Rava and Shmuel
          identify three places — the ash-mound, the golden vine at the Sanctuary entrance, and the Curtain
          separating the Sanctuary from the Holy of Holies — where the Sages described real Temple objects using
          deliberate exaggeration, and Rabbi Ami generalizes the principle to the Torah and the Prophets as well
          (<a class="!text-[#a9c4ff] underline hover:!text-[#cddcff]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.90b.9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">90b:9–15</a>).
          The daf closes back on the mishna itself: it forbids gid hanasheh in both thighs, against Rabbi Yehuda's
          view that only one thigh is forbidden — and the Gemara can't quite settle whether Rabbi Yehuda was
          certain which one or only guessing, before Rav Ika bar Chanina reframes the one piece of proof-text that
          seemed to decide it
          (<a class="!text-[#a9c4ff] underline hover:!text-[#cddcff]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.90b.16" target="_blank" rel="noopener">90b:16–21</a>).
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.90a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#7fa2e8]/40 bg-[#7fa2e8]/10 px-3 py-1.5 text-xs font-bold !text-[#a9c4ff] hover:bg-[#7fa2e8]/20">Chullin 90 on Sefaria →</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rashi_on_Chullin.90a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#1c2648] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#8fa0c9] hover:!text-[#dfe6f7]">Rashi (×60)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Steinsaltz_on_Chullin.90a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#1c2648] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#8fa0c9] hover:!text-[#dfe6f7]">Steinsaltz (×39)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rabbeinu_Gershom_on_Chullin.90a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#1c2648] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#8fa0c9] hover:!text-[#dfe6f7]">Rabbeinu Gershom (×26)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Ritva_on_Chullin.90a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#1c2648] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#8fa0c9] hover:!text-[#dfe6f7]">Ritva (×9)</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <style>
    .frank {
      font-family: 'Frank Ruhl Libre', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    }
    .he-inline { direction: rtl; font-family: 'Frank Ruhl Libre', serif; }
    /* ---- Activity 1: routing cards ---- */
    .route-card {
      border: 1px solid #161e3a;
      background: #0b0f20;
      border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 12px;
    }
    .route-btns { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; margin-top: 8px; }
    .route-btn {
      border: 1px solid #1c2648;
      background: #0f142a;
      color: #dfe6f7;
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 7px 12px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .route-btn:hover { border-color: #7fa2e8; color: #a9c4ff; }
    .route-btn:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.5; }
    .route-correct { border-color: #5a9e6f !important; background: #0d1c12 !important; color: #a9dcb8 !important; }
    .route-wrong { border-color: #b0604f !important; background: #201009 !important; color: #e2a58f !important; }
    .route-explain { margin-top: 8px; font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.55; color: #93a2c9; }
    /* ---- Activity 2: guzma chips ---- */
    .guzma-card {
      border: 1px solid #241a08;
      background: #120d04;
      border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 12px;
    }
    .guzma-title { font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; color: #ffcf8a; }
    .guzma-ref { font-size: 10px; color: #a3813f; margin-left: 6px; }
    .guzma-chips { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px; margin-top: 8px; }
    .guzma-chip {
      border: 1px solid #2a2010;
      background: #0f0a03;
      color: #e0cdb0;
      font-size: 12px;
      line-height: 1.4;
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 8px 12px;
      cursor: pointer;
      text-align: left;
    }
    .guzma-chip:hover { border-color: #e8a34d; }
    .guzma-chip:disabled { cursor: default; }
    .guzma-chip.chip-guzma-correct { border-color: #e8a34d !important; background: #2a1c08 !important; color: #ffcf8a !important; }
    .guzma-chip.chip-real { border-color: #3a4a1c !important; background: #131c0c !important; color: #c3dba0 !important; opacity: 0.85; }
    .guzma-chip.chip-missed { border-color: #b0604f !important; background: #201009 !important; color: #e2a58f !important; }
    .guzma-explain { margin-top: 8px; font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.55; color: #cbb589; }
    /* ---- Activity 3: thigh dilemma steps ---- */
    .thigh-btn {
      border: 1px solid #1c2648;
      background: #0b0f20;
      color: #a9c4ff;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 10px 14px;
      cursor: pointer;
      width: 100%;
      text-align: left;
    }
    .thigh-btn:hover { border-color: #7fa2e8; }
    .thigh-btn:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.55; }
    .thigh-text {
      border: 1px solid #161e3a;
      background: #0b0f20;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 10px 14px;
      font-size: 12px;
      line-height: 1.6;
      color: #c3cde6;
    }
    .thigh-choice { display: flex; gap: 8px; margin-top: 8px; }
    .thigh-choice-btn {
      border: 1px solid #1c2648;
      background: #0f142a;
      color: #dfe6f7;
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 7px 14px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .thigh-choice-btn:hover { border-color: #7fa2e8; color: #a9c4ff; }
    .thigh-choice-btn:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.6; }
    .thigh-choice-btn.chosen { border-color: #7fa2e8; background: #141c38; color: #a9c4ff; }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .fade-in { animation: fadeIn 0.3s ease both; }
      @keyframes fadeIn { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(4px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: none; } }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id);
    const $$ = (sel: string, root: ParentNode = document) => Array.from(root.querySelectorAll(sel));

    // ==================================================================
    // Activity 1 — Route the Gid, Chullin 90a:8-90b:8. Seven real cases:
    // where does each piece of a sacrifice end up? Ascend with the rest,
    // land on the tapuach (ash-mound, reserved for one specific case), or
    // come off the altar entirely.
    // ==================================================================
    type Dest = 'ascend' | 'tapuach' | 'off';
    type RouteCase = { he: string; label: string; correct: Dest; why: string; ref: string };
    const ROUTES: RouteCase[] = [
      {
        he: 'גִּיד הַנָּשֶׁה שֶׁל שְׁלָמִים',
        label: 'The sciatic nerve (gid hanasheh) of a peace-offering (shelamim)',
        correct: 'off',
        why: 'Swept into the Temple courtyard drain. It is forbidden to eat and was never meant for the altar at all.',
        ref: 'Chullin 90b:6, 8',
      },
      {
        he: 'גִּיד הַנָּשֶׁה שֶׁל עוֹלָה',
        label: 'The sciatic nerve (gid hanasheh) of a burnt-offering (olah)',
        correct: 'tapuach',
        why: "Rav Huna's ruling, confirmed by a concurring baraita: removed and set on the round ash-mound at the altar's center — not burned with the rest, and not simply swept away like the shelamim's.",
        ref: 'Chullin 90b:4, 8',
      },
      {
        he: 'עֲצָמוֹת וְגִידִין הַמְחוּבָּרִים לַבָּשָׂר',
        label: 'Bones and sinews still attached to the flesh of a burnt-offering',
        correct: 'ascend',
        why: "Obvious even without a verse, the Rabbis say — just like the head of the olah, which the Torah has ascend whole.",
        ref: 'Chullin 90a:17',
      },
      {
        he: 'רֹאשׁ הָעוֹלָה',
        label: 'The head of a burnt-offering, full of bones',
        correct: 'ascend',
        why: '"And Aaron\'s sons, the priests, shall lay the pieces, and the head, and the fat, in order..." — the head ascends whole with the rest of the animal.',
        ref: 'Leviticus 1:8; Chullin 90a:17',
      },
      {
        he: 'עֲצָמוֹת וְגִידִין שֶׁפֵּרְשׁוּ',
        label: 'Bones and sinews that came loose from the flesh — even after already reaching the top of the altar',
        correct: 'off',
        why: "Rebbi's explicit ruling: once detached, they come back down, even from the very head of the altar.",
        ref: 'Chullin 90a:13, 16',
      },
      {
        he: 'עִיכּוּלֵי בָשָׂר שֶׁנִּיתְּזוּ',
        label: 'Partly-burnt meat of a burnt-offering that falls off the altar',
        correct: 'ascend',
        why: 'You return it to the fire — meat keeps its status even mid-consumption.',
        ref: 'Chullin 90a:15',
      },
      {
        he: 'עִיכּוּלֵי גִידִין וַעֲצָמוֹת שֶׁנִּיתְּזוּ',
        label: 'Partly-burnt sinews and bones (not gid hanasheh) that fall off the altar',
        correct: 'off',
        why: 'Unlike meat, these are not returned — the baraita draws this exact contrast between meat and bones/sinews.',
        ref: 'Chullin 90a:15',
      },
    ];
    const DEST_LABEL: Record<Dest, string> = { ascend: '⬆ Ascends', tapuach: '🔥 Ash-mound', off: '⛔ Off the altar' };

    const routeList = $('route-list');
    let routedCount = 0;
    if (routeList) {
      ROUTES.forEach((rc) => {
        const card = document.createElement('div');
        card.className = 'route-card';
        card.innerHTML = `
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#dfe6f7]">${rc.label}</p>
          <p class="he-inline mt-1 text-xs !text-[#7fa2e8]" dir="rtl">${rc.he}</p>
          <div class="route-btns">
            <button class="route-btn" data-d="ascend">⬆ Ascends</button>
            <button class="route-btn" data-d="tapuach">🔥 Ash-mound</button>
            <button class="route-btn" data-d="off">⛔ Off the altar</button>
          </div>
          <div class="route-explain hidden"></div>
        `;
        routeList.appendChild(card);
        let answered = false;
        $$('.route-btn', card).forEach((btn) => {
          btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
            if (answered) return;
            answered = true;
            const chosen = btn.getAttribute('data-d') as Dest;
            $$('.route-btn', card).forEach((b) => {
              (b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true;
              const bVal = b.getAttribute('data-d') as Dest;
              if (bVal === rc.correct) b.classList.add('route-correct');
              else if (b === btn) b.classList.add('route-wrong');
            });
            const explain = card.querySelector('.route-explain');
            if (explain) {
              explain.classList.remove('hidden');
              explain.classList.add('fade-in');
              const verdict = chosen === rc.correct
                ? '<strong style="color:#a9dcb8">Correct.</strong>'
                : `<strong style="color:#e2a58f">Not quite — it's ${DEST_LABEL[rc.correct]}.</strong>`;
              explain.innerHTML = `${verdict} ${rc.why} (${rc.ref})`;
            }
            routedCount += 1;
            const scoreEl = $('score-route');
            if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `routed ${routedCount}/${ROUTES.length}`;
          });
        });
      });
    }

    // ==================================================================
    // Activity 2 — Spot the 300, Chullin 90b:9-15. Three real mishnayot,
    // each with exactly one detail flagged as guzma (exaggeration) by Rava
    // or Shmuel, hiding among otherwise-real measurements.
    // ==================================================================
    type Chip = { text: string; guzma: boolean; note?: string };
    type GuzmaRound = { title: string; ref: string; chips: Chip[] };
    const GUZMA_ROUNDS: GuzmaRound[] = [
      {
        title: 'The Ash-Mound (תפוח)',
        ref: 'Chullin 90b:9, citing Tamid 28b',
        chips: [
          { text: 'A mound of ash sat in the center of the altar', guzma: false },
          { text: 'It grew from the daily ash of the offerings burned there', guzma: false },
          { text: 'Sometimes it held as much as three hundred kor of ash', guzma: true, note: 'Rava: guzma — meant to convey "a great deal," not a literal measure. (Chullin 90b:9)' },
        ],
      },
      {
        title: 'The Golden Vine (גֶּפֶן)',
        ref: 'Chullin 90b:14, citing Middot 36a',
        chips: [
          { text: 'A golden vine stood at the entrance to the Sanctuary, hung on poles', guzma: false },
          { text: 'Worshippers who donated a golden leaf, grape, or cluster hung it on the vine', guzma: false },
          { text: 'Once, three hundred priests were needed to move it', guzma: true, note: 'Told by Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Tzadok — and named by Shmuel as guzma, not a literal headcount. (Chullin 90b:14)' },
        ],
      },
      {
        title: 'The Curtain (פָּרוֹכֶת)',
        ref: 'Chullin 90b:15, citing Shekalim 8:2',
        chips: [
          { text: 'It was a handbreadth (tefach) thick', guzma: false },
          { text: 'Woven of seventy-two strands, each of twenty-four threads', guzma: false },
          { text: "Forty cubits long and twenty cubits wide — the Sanctuary's own dimensions", guzma: false },
          { text: 'Cost 820,000 dinars, and two were woven every year', guzma: false },
          { text: 'Three hundred priests were needed to immerse it', guzma: true, note: "Shmuel's third guzma — the fabric's measurements stand, but no curtain needs three hundred men to dunk it in a mikveh. (Chullin 90b:15)" },
        ],
      },
    ];

    const guzmaWrap = $('guzma-rounds');
    let guzmaDone = 0;
    if (guzmaWrap) {
      GUZMA_ROUNDS.forEach((round) => {
        const card = document.createElement('div');
        card.className = 'guzma-card';
        card.innerHTML = `
          <span class="guzma-title">${round.title}</span><span class="guzma-ref">${round.ref}</span>
          <div class="guzma-chips"></div>
          <div class="guzma-explain hidden"></div>
        `;
        guzmaWrap.appendChild(card);
        const chipsWrap = card.querySelector('.guzma-chips');
        let answered = false;
        round.chips.forEach((chip, i) => {
          const btn = document.createElement('button');
          btn.className = 'guzma-chip';
          btn.textContent = chip.text;
          chipsWrap?.appendChild(btn);
          btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
            if (answered) return;
            answered = true;
            const correctChip = round.chips.find((c) => c.guzma)!;
            $$('.guzma-chip', card).forEach((b, j) => {
              (b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true;
              if (round.chips[j].guzma) b.classList.add('chip-guzma-correct');
              else b.classList.add('chip-real');
            });
            if (!chip.guzma) btn.classList.add('chip-missed');
            const explain = card.querySelector('.guzma-explain');
            if (explain) {
              explain.classList.remove('hidden');
              explain.classList.add('fade-in');
              const verdict = chip.guzma
                ? '<strong style="color:#ffcf8a">Found it.</strong>'
                : '<strong style="color:#e2a58f">That one is real.</strong>';
              explain.innerHTML = `${verdict} ${correctChip.note}`;
            }
            guzmaDone += 1;
            const scoreEl = $('score-guzma');
            if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `guzma ${guzmaDone}/${GUZMA_ROUNDS.length}`;
            if (guzmaDone === GUZMA_ROUNDS.length) {
              const bonus = $('guzma-bonus');
              if (bonus) {
                bonus.classList.remove('hidden');
                bonus.classList.add('fade-in');
                bonus.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'nearest' });
              }
            }
          });
        });
      });
    }

    // ==================================================================
    // Activity 3 — One Thigh or Two?, Chullin 90b:16-21. Rabbi Yehuda
    // holds gid hanasheh applies to only one thigh, "and the mind decides
    // for the right." The Gemara investigates whether he was certain or
    // unsure, then Rav Ika bar Chanina reframes the one proof that seemed
    // to decide it.
    // ==================================================================
    const THIGH_STEPS: { prompt: string; reveal: string; choice?: boolean }[] = [
      {
        prompt: 'The mishna forbids gid hanasheh in the right thigh AND the left thigh. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: "it applies only to one of them, and the mind decides for the right." Tap to see the dilemma this provokes.',
        reveal: 'A dilemma was raised before the Sages: is Rabbi Yehuda <em>certain</em> it\'s the right thigh — so "the mind decides" invokes the Torah\'s own settled logic? Or is he genuinely <em>unsure</em> whether it\'s the right or the left — so "the mind decides" is just a leaning, a best guess? (Chullin 90b:17)',
      },
      {
        prompt: 'Before the Gemara searches for proof — take a side. Was Rabbi Yehuda certain, or unsure?',
        reveal: 'Held that thought? Good — because the Gemara now goes looking for a source to settle it, and the answer isn\'t where you\'d expect.',
        choice: true,
      },
      {
        prompt: 'The Gemara reaches for a mishna about the Korban Pesach (Pesachim 83a): its bones, sinews, and leftover meat are burned on the sixteenth of Nisan. But what "sinews" could that mean — ordinary meat-sinews would just be eaten, and leftover meat is already covered by "leftover." Tap to see Rav Chisda\'s answer.',
        reveal: 'Rav Chisda: those "sinews" are the gid hanasheh — and the mishna is speaking specifically according to Rabbi Yehuda\'s one-thigh view. (Chullin 90b:18–19)',
      },
      {
        prompt: 'Does Rav Chisda\'s proof favor "certain" or "unsure"? Tap to see the Gemara\'s own analysis.',
        reveal: 'It fits perfectly if Rabbi Yehuda was <strong>unsure</strong> which thigh: you can\'t eat either gid on the doubt, so by the sixteenth both must be burned as doubtful leftover meat. But if he were <strong>certain</strong> it\'s the right thigh, he should simply eat the permitted left one\'s gid and just discard — not burn — the forbidden right one\'s. Taken at face value, the proof favors "unsure." (Chullin 90b:20)',
      },
      {
        prompt: 'Rav Ika bar Chanina has the last word. Tap to see how he rescues "certain."',
        reveal: 'Rav Ika bar Chanina: actually, Rabbi Yehuda could still be certain. The Pesachim case is different — the right and left sciatic nerves were correctly identified and separated at first, but then got mixed together, so no one can tell which is which anymore. Both are burned not because the law itself is in doubt, but because these particular pieces are. The Gemara records no final verdict on Rabbi Yehuda\'s original certainty — only that the "certain" reading survives the challenge. (Chullin 90b:21)',
      },
    ];
    const thighWrap = $('thigh-steps');
    let thighDone = 0;
    if (thighWrap) {
      THIGH_STEPS.forEach((step, i) => {
        const row = document.createElement('div');
        if (step.choice) {
          row.innerHTML = `
            <p class="thigh-btn" style="cursor:default;" data-locked="${i > 0 ? '1' : '0'}">${step.prompt}</p>
            <div class="thigh-choice">
              <button class="thigh-choice-btn" data-c="certain" ${i > 0 ? 'disabled' : ''}>Certain</button>
              <button class="thigh-choice-btn" data-c="unsure" ${i > 0 ? 'disabled' : ''}>Unsure</button>
            </div>
            <div class="thigh-text hidden mt-2"></div>
          `;
        } else {
          row.innerHTML = `
            <button class="thigh-btn" ${i > 0 ? 'disabled' : ''}>${step.prompt}</button>
            <div class="thigh-text hidden mt-2"></div>
          `;
        }
        thighWrap.appendChild(row);
        const text = row.querySelector('.thigh-text');

        function advance() {
          thighDone += 1;
          const scoreEl = $('score-thigh');
          if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `thigh steps ${thighDone}/${THIGH_STEPS.length}`;
          const nextRow = thighWrap.children[i + 1];
          if (nextRow) {
            const nextBtn = nextRow.querySelector('.thigh-btn:not([style])') as HTMLButtonElement | null;
            if (nextBtn) nextBtn.disabled = false;
            $$('.thigh-choice-btn', nextRow as HTMLElement).forEach((b) => ((b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = false));
          }
        }

        if (step.choice) {
          $$('.thigh-choice-btn', row).forEach((btn) => {
            btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
              if ((btn as HTMLButtonElement).disabled) return;
              $$('.thigh-choice-btn', row).forEach((b) => {
                (b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true;
              });
              btn.classList.add('chosen');
              if (text) {
                text.classList.remove('hidden');
                text.classList.add('fade-in');
                text.innerHTML = step.reveal;
              }
              advance();
            });
          });
        } else {
          const btn = row.querySelector('.thigh-btn') as HTMLButtonElement;
          btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
            btn.disabled = true;
            if (text) {
              text.classList.remove('hidden');
              text.classList.add('fade-in');
              text.innerHTML = step.reveal;
            }
            advance();
          });
        }
      });
    }
  </script>
</Layout>
